The original problem text: "¬(A ∧ B) ↔ (¬A ∨ ¬B) ¬(A ∨ B) ↔ (¬A ∧ ¬B) (A → B) ↔ (¬B → ¬A) (A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B) (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B) ¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B) Which of the above is not logically equivalent to the rest? Provide the full statement that doesn't fit."

The conclusion from subtask 1 (Reasoner) is: "The biconditional statement (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B) is not a tautology because the left and right sides are not logically equivalent. For example, when A is false and B is true, ¬A → B is true, but A ∨ ¬B is false. The other five statements are well-known logical equivalences (De Morgan's laws, contrapositive, implication to disjunction, and negation of implication). Thus, the statement that does not fit is: (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)""}

